# Zustand State Management Best Practices

## Store Structure

- Create separate stores for different domains (auth, UI, data)
- Keep stores focused and single-purpose
- Co-locate store with related components when appropriate
- Use a `stores/` or `lib/store/` directory for shared stores
- Export both the hook and the store instance
- Use TypeScript for type-safe stores
- Document store purpose and usage

## Store Definition

- Use `create` from zustand
- Define proper TypeScript interfaces for state and actions
- Keep state flat when possible
- Group related state together
- Separate state and actions clearly
- Use descriptive action names
- Implement proper action signatures
- Return new objects/arrays for immutability

## TypeScript Patterns

- Define state interface before creating store
- Use proper typing for actions
- Leverage TypeScript inference
- Use generics when appropriate
- Type selector functions properly
- Use `StoreApi` type for advanced patterns
- Avoid `any` types in stores
- Export store types for reuse

## Client Components Only

- Zustand only works in Client Components
- Mark components using Zustand with 'use client'
- Don't use Zustand in Server Components
- Initialize stores in client components
- Pass initial data from server to client when needed
- Consider using React Context for server-rendered state
- Hydrate stores carefully with server data

## State Updates

- Use set() to update state
- Keep updates simple and focused
- Avoid nested updates when possible
- Use immer middleware for complex nested updates
- Batch related updates together
- Use functional updates when depending on previous state
- Don't mutate state directly
- Return new state objects

## Selectors

- Use selectors to prevent unnecessary re-renders
- Keep selectors simple and focused
- Use shallow equality for object/array selectors
- Create reusable selector functions
- Use `useShallow` hook for automatic shallow comparison
- Avoid selecting entire store unless necessary
- Memoize complex selectors
- Test selector performance

## Derived State

- Compute derived state in selectors
- Don't store computed values in state
- Use selector functions for transformations
- Memoize expensive computations
- Keep derived logic in selectors, not components
- Consider using separate selectors for different views
- Test derived state logic

## Actions

- Define actions inside the store
- Keep actions focused and single-purpose
- Use descriptive action names (e.g., `addTodo`, `setUser`)
- Handle async operations in actions
- Implement error handling in actions
- Return meaningful values from actions when needed
- Don't expose `set` directly outside the store
- Log actions in development when needed

## Async Operations

- Handle async logic in actions
- Set loading states before async operations
- Handle errors appropriately
- Update state after successful operations
- Clear loading states in finally blocks
- Implement proper error messages
- Use try-catch blocks
- Consider using middleware for async patterns

## Middleware

- Use `persist` middleware for localStorage persistence
- Use `devtools` middleware in development
- Use `immer` middleware for complex state updates
- Chain middleware properly
- Configure middleware options appropriately
- Use `subscribeWithSelector` for selective subscriptions
- Implement custom middleware when needed
- Test middleware behavior

## Persistence

- Use persist middleware for client-side persistence
- Configure storage (localStorage, sessionStorage)
- Serialize/deserialize properly
- Handle migration for schema changes
- Version your persisted state
- Clear persisted state when needed
- Handle hydration carefully
- Don't persist sensitive data

## Performance

- Use selectors to minimize re-renders
- Split large stores into smaller ones
- Use shallow equality checking
- Avoid selecting unnecessary state
- Memoize expensive computations
- Profile and optimize hot paths
- Use devtools to monitor performance
- Test with React DevTools Profiler

## Integration with Supabase

- Don't store sensitive Supabase data in Zustand
- Use Zustand for UI state, not auth state
- Sync Supabase data to Zustand when needed
- Handle real-time updates in store actions
- Clear store on logout
- Use Supabase as source of truth
- Cache Supabase data appropriately
- Handle offline scenarios

## Testing Stores

- Test stores in isolation
- Test actions and state updates
- Test async operations
- Test error handling
- Mock external dependencies
- Test persistence behavior
- Test selectors
- Use act() for async updates in tests

## Common Patterns

### UI State Store
```typescript
interface UIState {
  isModalOpen: boolean
  modalContent: string | null
  openModal: (content: string) => void
  closeModal: () => void
}
```

### Data Cache Store
```typescript
interface DataState<T> {
  data: T | null
  loading: boolean
  error: string | null
  fetchData: () => Promise<void>
  clearData: () => void
}
```

### Form State Store
```typescript
interface FormState {
  values: Record<string, any>
  errors: Record<string, string>
  setValue: (key: string, value: any) => void
  setError: (key: string, error: string) => void
  reset: () => void
}
```

## Store Organization

- Group related state and actions together
- Separate concerns into different stores
- Create a root store if needed for combining stores
- Use store composition for complex scenarios
- Keep stores independent when possible
- Share state through props when appropriate
- Document store dependencies

## DevTools Integration

- Enable Redux DevTools in development
- Use meaningful action names for debugging
- Inspect state changes in DevTools
- Time-travel debug when needed
- Monitor performance in DevTools
- Use DevTools for testing state changes
- Disable in production for performance

## Hydration

- Handle Next.js hydration carefully
- Use useEffect for initial hydration
- Don't access store during SSR
- Initialize stores on client mount
- Handle hydration mismatches
- Use suspense boundaries if needed
- Test hydration scenarios

## Security

- Don't store sensitive data in Zustand
- Clear sensitive state on logout
- Don't persist authentication tokens
- Validate data before storing
- Sanitize user input
- Use Supabase for sensitive operations
- Clear state on auth changes

## Migration

- Version your store schema
- Implement migration functions for persist
- Handle missing persisted data gracefully
- Clear incompatible persisted state
- Document schema changes
- Test migrations thoroughly
- Provide fallbacks for old data

## Best Practices

- Keep stores simple and focused
- Use TypeScript for type safety
- Test store logic thoroughly
- Document complex stores
- Use middleware appropriately
- Profile performance regularly
- Follow naming conventions
- Handle errors gracefully
- Clear state when appropriate
- Use selectors to prevent re-renders
- Keep stores client-side only
- Use Supabase for persistence, Zustand for UI state
